From: WinBoat
Subject: [PATCH] viogpu3d: WDDM 2.0 kernel driver with GPU virtual addresses

Windows' Direct3D 12 runtime only takes adapters whose kernel driver is
WDDM 2.0 or newer. The driver now reports WDDM 2.0 (DXGKDDI_WDDMv2) with
GPU virtual addresses (GpuMmu); building with VIOGPU_WDDM1_3, or with a WDK
older than WDDM 2.0 (Windows 8.1), keeps the WDDM 1.3 driver.

- GpuMmu with page tables the CPU updates (DXGK_PAGETABLEUPDATE_CPU_VIRTUAL):
  3 levels of 512 entries, 39-bit addresses, page tables in the system
  memory segment. The virtual GPU never reads them (its commands name host
  resources), so UPDATE_PAGE_TABLE copies the entries and FLUSH_TLB,
  UPDATE_CONTEXT_ALLOCATION, NOTIFY_RESIDENCY and DISCARD_CONTENT do nothing.
- QUERYSEGMENT4 (the WDDM 1.3 aperture segment), GPUMMUCAPS,
  PAGETABLELEVELDESC; CreateProcess/DestroyProcess, SetRootPageTable,
  GetRootPageTableSize, CalibrateGpuClock (the performance counter),
  SetStablePowerState (required: StartAdapter fails without it) and
  SubmitCommandVirtual.
- The allocations the D3D/OpenGL drivers use are accessed physically and
  stay in the aperture segment; DMA buffers too (in plain system memory the
  memory manager crashed setting up the GDI context's pool).
- Handle data through DxgkCbAcquireHandleData (DxgkCbGetHandleData returns
  NULL under WDDM 2.0); OpenAllocation skips allocations it can't resolve.
- VIOGPU_ADAPTERINFO::Flags.Wddm2 tells the user-mode drivers to make their
  allocations resident (MakeResident), which WDDM 2.0 leaves to them.
- Preemption (also a fix for WDDM 1.3): the driver said PreemptionAware but
  DxgkDdiPreemptCommand did nothing, and the scheduler waits for
  DXGK_INTERRUPT_DMA_PREEMPTED, so a preemption request stalled the whole
  GPU (presents and context destruction hung). The host runs each submitted
  command to its end, so the request completes once the commands submitted
  before it are done, with nothing to resubmit.
- VIOGPU_RES_BUSY waits are bounded (10 s): a command buffer Windows drops
  after Render never completes, and an endless wait blocked every later
  escape of the adapter.
- A small diagnostic log in memory (VioGpuLog, also to DbgPrintEx), read
  with the VIOGPU_GET_LOG escape; it records rare events only.

DirectX test on the WDDM 2.0 driver: 514 passed, 1 failed (Direct3D 12, no
user-mode driver yet), as on WDDM 1.3; the device restarts and updates
in place.
---
diff --git a/viogpu/common/helper.h b/viogpu/common/helper.h
--- a/viogpu/common/helper.h
+++ b/viogpu/common/helper.h
@@ -100,6 +100,28 @@
     }
 #endif
 
+// WDDM 2.0 with GPU virtual addresses (GpuMmu): what Direct3D 12 needs. Building with VIOGPU_WDDM1_3 (or with a WDK
+// older than WDDM 2.0, for Windows 8.1) keeps the WDDM 1.3 driver
+#if !defined(VIOGPU_WDDM1_3) && (DXGKDDI_INTERFACE_VERSION >= DXGKDDI_INTERFACE_VERSION_WDDM2_0)
+#define VIOGPU_WDDM2 1
+#else
+#define VIOGPU_WDDM2 0
+#endif
+
+// GPU page tables (VIOGPU_WDDM2): 3 levels of 512 entries (DXGK_PTE, 16 bytes: 8 KB a table) over 4 KB pages, a
+// 39-bit (512 GB) GPU virtual address space. The CPU updates them (DXGK_PAGETABLEUPDATE_CPU_VIRTUAL) and the virtual
+// GPU never reads them: its commands name host resources, not addresses
+#define VIOGPU_PAGE_TABLE_LEVELS     3
+#define VIOGPU_PAGE_TABLE_INDEX_BITS 9
+#define VIOGPU_PAGE_TABLE_SIZE       ((1 << VIOGPU_PAGE_TABLE_INDEX_BITS) * 16)
+#define VIOGPU_GPU_VA_BITS           (VIOGPU_PAGE_TABLE_LEVELS * VIOGPU_PAGE_TABLE_INDEX_BITS + 12)
+// The aperture segment is segment 1, so the system memory segment GpuMmu adds is segment 2
+#define VIOGPU_SYSMEM_SEGMENT_ID     2
+
+// A diagnostic log kept in memory (32 KB, oldest lines dropped), read with the VIOGPU_GET_LOG escape. Any IRQL up to
+// DISPATCH_LEVEL
+void VioGpuLog(_In_z_ _Printf_format_string_ const char *format, ...);
+
 #define PAGED_CODE_SEG       __declspec(code_seg("PAGE"))
 #define PAGED_CODE_SEG_BEGIN __pragma(code_seg(push)) __pragma(code_seg("PAGE"))
 
